  {
   "text": "LLANGOLLEN. Croeso i Langollen. Welcome to the town where everyone sings and nobody admits it.",
   "x": 3.0,
   "y": 17.0
  },
  {
   "text": "RAILWAY. Steam trains up the valley. Tickets from the man in the hat.",
   "x": 44.0,
   "y": 23.0
  },
  {
   "text": "DINAS BRÂN. The castle on the hill. 'Fortress of the crow'. Crows have the lease.",
   "x": 14.0,
   "y": 32.0
  },
  {
   "text": "EAST: Dee Valley and the Aqueduct. Bring a head for heights, or a boat.",
   "x": 60.0,
   "y": 16.0
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Eleanor Butler and Sarah Ponsonby, the Ladies of Llangollen, made their home at Plas Newydd in 1780.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "llangollen_ladies",
   "title": "Ladies of Llangollen"
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "llangollen_name",
   "title": "Name: Llangollen",
   "text": "Llangollen is the llan, or church, of St Collen. In legend Collen met Gwyn ap Nudd, king of the fairies, on Glastonbury Tor, sprinkled holy water about, and the whole fairy court vanished. After that he settled here, somewhere quieter."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 28,
   "local": {"cy": "Abaty Glyn y Groes"},
   "plaque": "llangollen_valle_crucis",
   "title": "Valle Crucis Abbey",
   "text": "Just north of town lie the ruins of Valle Crucis Abbey, founded in 1201 by Madog ap Gruffudd, prince of northern Powys, for Cistercian monks. The Latin name means 'valley of the cross', after Eliseg's Pillar, a cross raised nearby in the 800s. The monks kept a fishpond, which is still there."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "llangollen_bridge",
   "title": "Llangollen Bridge",
   "text": "The old stone bridge over the Dee is one of the Seven Wonders of Wales, in a rhyme that also lists Snowdon, Pistyll Rhaeadr, Wrexham steeple, Overton yews, St Winefride's Well and Gresford bells. Most of them are in north east Wales. The poet may not have travelled much."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "llangollen_glyndwr",
   "title": "Owain Glyndŵr",
   "text": "On 16 September 1400, at Glyndyfrdwy a few miles up the Dee, Owain Glyndŵr was proclaimed Prince of Wales and began the last great Welsh rising against English rule. His name comes from those lands, the glen of the Dee. 16 September is still Owain Glyndŵr Day."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "arts_triple_harp",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Triple Harp",
   "text": "The telyn deires, the triple harp, has three rows of strings, the middle row the sharps and flats, so a player can manage any key by reaching through. It arrived from Italy by way of London in the 1600s and became the harp of Wales. Nansi Richards of Pen-y-bont-fawr, 'Telynores Maldwyn', kept the old way of playing alive into the 1970s and taught it on. Cerdd dant, singing a counter-tune against the harp's air, is still judged at eisteddfodau. The International Eisteddfod has sung here every July since 1947."
  }
 ],
